## SECTION B — (MID-PEEL): INDEPENDENT RE-DERIVATION AND THE WELL-FOUNDEDNESS AUDIT

B.1  What I re-derived (all four steps check out)

  State: current monic key Phi~ of degree D, monic separable represented
  G = Phi~^nu + sum_{j<nu} C_j Phi~^j, deg C_j < D, deg G = nu*D.

  (1) TRIGGER IS DECIDABLE AND EXACT.  G mod Phi~ = C_0 (deg C_0 < D), so
      Phi~ | G  <=>  C_0 = 0. This is the exact level-one/level-two "A_0 = 0"
      criterion (HE6 sec-S1 CONVENTION L84-85: "Phi' does not divide f,
      equivalently A_0 != 0"; HE7 R2 L2340-2341). The note's phrasing "test
      whether Phi~ | G" is therefore effective, not oracular.
  (2) THE QUOTIENT'S DEVELOPMENT AND (MID-MASS).  With C_0 = 0,
      G = Phi~*(Phi~^{nu-1} + sum_{i<nu-1} C_{i+1} Phi~^i), so G' := G/Phi~ is
      monic in O[x] (Phi~ monic), deg G' = D(nu-1), and its Phi~-development is
      the shift C_j -> C_{j+1} with every deg C_{i+1} < D. So mu(G') = nu - 1
      EXACTLY, not merely <=. (MID-MASS) CONFIRMED.
  (3) KEY-FREENESS OF THE QUOTIENT.  If an irreducible p in K_0[x] divided both
      G' and Phi~, then p^2 | Phi~*G' = G, contradicting G separable. So
      gcd_{K_0[x]}(G', Phi~) = 1. CONFIRMED, and note this is genuinely the full
      gcd condition, not just Phi~ ∤ G' -- so the quotient satisfies (KEY-FREE)
      even at a reducible key, which is what the master needs.
      Corollary the note does not state but which is used implicitly:
      roots(G) = roots(Phi~) DISJOINT-UNION roots(G'), so the peel neither loses
      nor duplicates roots.
  (4) (WINDOW) IS NOT AUTOMATICALLY RESTORED, AND THE NOTE KNOWS IT.  The peel
      removes the d(Phi~(rho)) = +infinity roots, but "T < d(Phi~(rho))" for the
      surviving roots is an extra fact. r3 repair 4 item 2 demands exactly it as
      a certified input:
          "2. `(WINDOW)` for \(G'\): every root \(\rho\) of \(G'\) satisfies
          \(T<d(\widetilde\Phi(\rho))<\infty\);"
      CORRECT AND CORRECTLY FENCED. The r3r4 suite (items 1-5) is the right
      closure of the F5 gap: without item 5 in particular (the peeled key's
      certified orbit/(e,f) decomposition) the peel would silently assume the
      monic key irreducible, which the note elsewhere forbids.
  (5) nu = 1 GUARD (r3 repair 5). At nu = 1, C_0 = 0 gives G = Phi~ and G' = 1;
      there is no successor and (MID-MASS)'s "next key-free state" would be
      vacuous. The guard is necessary and correctly placed immediately after
      (MID-MASS). CONFIRMED.
  (6) NO IMMEDIATE LOOP. Since gcd(G', Phi~) = 1, the peel cannot fire twice in
      a row at the same key. CONFIRMED.

B.2  FINDING B-1 (GAP) -- clause 5's trigger is narrower than the algorithm it
     is supposed to state, and excludes the only case its own cited instance
     discharge is about.

  Effective clause 5 of HE7.A (r2 repair 3):
      "> 5. if a RECENTERED current key divides its represented block, the
      algorithm performs `(MID-PEEL)` before asserting `(WINDOW)`. ..."
  Effective ledger item 15 (r2 repair 8 / S5):
      "> 15. A RECENTERED key-divides state invokes `(MID-PEEL)` before
      `(WINDOW)`; its mass decreases by one."
  (emphasis mine; the word is "recentered" in both.)

  But the section body it is meant to state is NOT restricted to recentered keys:
      "> Let a recursive state have current monic key \(\widetilde\Phi\) of
      degree \(D\) and monic separable represented polynomial G = ... Before
      invoking the window, test whether \(\widetilde\Phi\mid G\)."
  and the cited level-two discharge is a CHILD-KEY case, not a recentering:
      r3 repair 4: "At level two the instance discharge is **LEMMA HE6R1-3**:
      \(\Psi\mid f_S\) forces \(\gcd(f_S,\Psi)=\Psi\) ..."
  Psi = Psi_{lambda,r} is the TEST POLYNOMIAL promoted to child key by S1.8's
  product->=2 branch. It is not a recentering of anything; S1.8's recentering is
  the separate product-1 branch "Phi_new = Phi - L_k(s)".

  Why it matters. Key-freeness is preserved by side and label splits (F_lambda,
  F_{lambda,r'} divide F, so gcd with Phi divides gcd(F,Phi) = 1), and is
  hypothesised at the top level by (KEY-FREE). The ONLY two transitions that can
  destroy it are (a) the product-1 recentering and (b) the product->=2 child
  jump. Clause 5 names (a) only. On the clause as written, a child state with
  Psi | f_child asserts (WINDOW) at roots with d(Psi(rho)) = +infinity, which is
  precisely the failure (WINDOW) exists to exclude -- and the source records that
  stratum as machine-real ("HE7's own sealed battery isolates the stratum as
  status `DEGENERATE-key-divides` (8 of 1,587 members)", HE7 L152-153).
  The general body and the induction paragraph ("In the mid-chain key-divides
  case, `(MID-PEEL)` emits ...", no "recentered") cover the case, so the
  mathematics is available; the THEOREM does not state it, and S7's stage-2 gate
  makes the theorem statement the formalization target.
  Classification: GAP.

B.3  FINDING B-2 (GAP) -- the rank is called "well-founded lexicographic" but the
     tuple is never displayed and the cross-component monotonicity is never
     checked; with three different quantities decreasing under four different
     transitions, that check is the whole content of the claim.

  Effective rank paragraph (r2 repair 3):
      "> The states carry a well-founded lexicographic rank. The permitted
      nonterminal transitions are:
      > 1. a nontrivial side split, which strictly decreases represented degree;
      > 2. a nontrivial label split, which strictly decreases represented degree;
      > 3. a mid-chain key-boundary peel `(MID-PEEL)`, which changes
      >    \[ \nu\longmapsto\nu-1; \]
      > 4. a product-\(\ge2\) child jump, which strictly decreases mass, or a
      >    product-\(1\) linear recentering, which strictly decreases the finite
      >    secondary discriminant/slope rank."

  A lexicographic order needs an ORDERED tuple, and each transition must decrease
  it lexicographically -- i.e. must not INCREASE any earlier component. Three
  distinct quantities appear (represented degree; mass; secondary rank) and no
  order among them is given, nor is any transition checked against the components
  it does not decrease. The gap is not academic: transition 4a decreases mass but
  is stated to say nothing about degree, and transition 4b preserves both degree
  and mass -- so on the text as written, nothing rules out a cycle
  (recentering -> child jump -> recentering -> ...) in which each step decreases
  "its own" quantity while an earlier one grows back.

  The claim IS repairable, and I verified the repair, which is what makes this a
  GAP rather than a CRITICAL. Take rank = (represented degree, mass, secondary),
  lexicographic. Then:
    - side split / label split: degree strictly down (each block has positive
      length and there are >= 2 of them).
    - (MID-PEEL): deg G' = D(nu-1) < D*nu, degree strictly down.
    - child jump: the child's degree is D_child * mu_child
      = (D e' deg r') * mu_child <= (D e' deg r') * mu/(e' deg r') = D*mu by
      (MASS), so degree does NOT increase, and mass strictly drops
      (mu_child <= mu/2 < mu at every nonterminal child).
    - linear recentering: root set preserved and D unchanged, so degree and mass
      are EQUAL, and the secondary rank strictly drops.
  Every transition is then a strict lex decrease and the order is well-founded on
  N^3. Two of the four facts I had to supply (the child jump's degree
  non-increase, via (MASS) composed with deg Psi = D e' deg r'; the recentering's
  degree/mass preservation) appear nowhere in the note.

  Note also that the repair is what makes the secondary rank's RESET across a
  peel harmless -- a point worth stating explicitly, since a peel plainly may
  reset the slope rank of the refine chain and the note offers no reason why
  that is safe.
  Classification: GAP.

B.4  (WINDOW) FINITENESS: VERDICT

  Given B.1 (1)-(6), r3r4's input suite, r3r5's nu = 1 guard, and B.3's supplied
  tuple, no state can loop and no infinite branch exists: peels are bounded by
  nu - 1 (each drops degree by D >= 1), splits are bounded by degree, child jumps
  by mass, recentering chains by the declared finite secondary rank, and the lex
  order sequences them. The (WINDOW) violation F3 identified (recentering makes
  the key DIVIDE the block; machine-real at 8/1587 states) IS closed at the level
  of the algorithm. It is not closed at the level of clause 5 (FINDING B-1) and
  the well-foundedness is asserted rather than shown (FINDING B-2).

