## SECTION E — MASTER CORE RE-DERIVED FROM THE EFFECTIVE TEXT ALONE

Decorrelation note: I derived both arguments before comparing with the note's own
proof text, and only then diffed. Both go through. The two defects I found are at
the joints (E.3, E.4), not in the core.

E.1  SINGLE-SIDE TERMINAL CASE (clauses 1 and 2)

  Data: F key-free sigma-block, F = Phi^mu + sum_{j<mu} A_j Phi^j, deg A_j < D,
  deg F = mu*D, roots Galois-stable in Pt, (WINDOW) T < d(Phi(rho)) < infinity,
  and the declared (ACCOUNT), (RES-DEG), (DEG-EF) D = e_C f_C, test assertions.

  Step 1 (hull spans [0,mu]).  gcd_{K_0[x]}(F,Phi) = 1 and deg Phi = D > 0 force
  A_0 != 0: F mod Phi = A_0 (deg A_0 < D), so A_0 = 0 would give Phi | F and
  gcd = Phi != 1. With A_mu = 1 the hull runs from abscissa 0 to abscissa mu, so
  sum_lambda L_lambda = mu.  [= (HULL-LENGTH), master's step 1.]

  Step 2 (no side of slope <= T).  Both sides of (ACCOUNT),
      sum_rho min(w_rho, kappa) = D sum_lambda L_lambda min(lambda, kappa),
  are continuous piecewise-linear in kappa, so equality off a finite set extends
  to every kappa > T. On (T, min_rho w_rho) -- nonempty by (WINDOW)'s STRICT
  T < w_rho and finiteness of the root set -- the left derivative is
  #{rho : w_rho > kappa} = mu*D and the right derivative is D sum_{lambda>kappa}
  L_lambda. Hence sum_{lambda>kappa} L_lambda = mu there; with all L_lambda > 0
  and sum over ALL sides equal to mu, no side has slope <= kappa, and letting
  kappa decrease to T, none has slope <= T.  CLAUSE 1 CONFIRMED.
  (The step consumes (WINDOW) twice -- finiteness for "piecewise linear with
  finitely many breaks", strictness for "the interval immediately above T is
  nonempty". Both are in (WINDOW) as stated.)

  Step 3 (SIDE-COUNT).  The derivative of the left side drops by exactly
  n_lambda := #{rho : w_rho = lambda} at kappa = lambda, and of the right side by
  D L_lambda. Equality of the two functions gives n_lambda = D L_lambda for every
  lambda (with L_lambda := 0 for a non-slope).  CONFIRMED.

  Step 4 (clause 2).  R_lambda separable => all parent multiplicities are 1, so
  (RES-DEG) reads e' sum_{r'} deg r' = L_lambda.  (DEG-SUM).
  Fix r' and an orbit Omega inside the class S_{lambda,r'}. F separable => rho is
  separable over K_0 => |Omega| = [K_0(rho):K_0]; K_0 complete discretely valued
  => the fundamental identity gives [K_0(rho):K_0] = e(rho/K_0) f(rho/K_0).
  Test assertion 6 (local forcing) gives e_C e' | e(rho/K_0) and
  f_C deg r' | f(rho/K_0), so with (DEG-EF)
      |Omega| >= (e_C e')(f_C deg r') = D e' deg r'.
  Hence |S_{lambda,r'}| >= D e' deg r' for each r' (classes nonempty), and summing
  with (DEG-SUM) gives sum_{r'} |S_{lambda,r'}| >= D L_lambda. The classes are
  disjoint and exhaust the side, whose cardinality is n_lambda = D L_lambda by
  Step 3; so the inequality is an equality termwise:
      |S_{lambda,r'}| = D e' deg r'.
  Every orbit in the class already has at least that many elements, so the class
  is a single orbit; its orbit polynomial is monic with Galois-fixed coefficients,
  integral over O, hence in O[x], divides the separable F, and is irreducible over
  K_0. Finally e f = D e' deg r' together with the two divisibilities forces
  e = e_C e' and f = f_C deg r'.  CLAUSE 2 CONFIRMED.

  Source cross-check (decorrelation): this is HE6 sec-S6.2's four-step sandwich
  (pin HE6-PROOF) generalized, and the divisibility form of local forcing that
  r2 repair 6 asserts really is what the sources prove --
      HE6_PROOF L416-424 (LEMMA HE6-0'): "e₁ | e(L_ρ) ... hence e₁ℓ | e(L_ρ) ...
      a subfield degree of the finite field k_{L_ρ} divides f(L_ρ)"
      HE7_PROOF L552-554 (LEMMA HE7-5): "then e₁ℓℓ₂ | e(L_ρ) and f₁d_rd_{r₂} |
      f(L_ρ); hence [L_ρ : K₀] ≥ D″ℓ₂d_{r₂}"
  so r2 repair 6's "Local forcing says e_C e' | e, f_C deg r' | f" is faithful and
  not an over-read. (I note the proof only needs ">=" at every step, including the
  final equality extraction; the divisibility is stronger than required but is
  genuinely available.)

E.2  (SIDE-PROD) MULTI-SIDE SPLIT

  The identity itself is DECLARED, not derived ("Let \(F\) be a state to which the
  carrier's block theorem applies. Its roots first decompose by their actual
  \(\Phi\)-value: F = prod_lambda F_lambda"), and this is honest: at level one it
  is (LB1) (Section D), at level two it is HE7-13'(a), whose product is over PAIRS
  (lambda, r) and therefore delivers the side grouping as well as the label
  grouping (HE7 L1680-1681: "f_S = Π_{(λ,r)} f_{λ,r}").
  What the master DOES have to derive, and what I checked, is that each F_lambda
  is a legitimate HE7.A state:
    - key-free: F_lambda | F, so gcd(F_lambda, Phi) | gcd(F, Phi) = 1.  OK
    - degree: its root set is the side-lambda roots of F, of cardinality
      n_lambda = D L_lambda by (SIDE-COUNT), so mu(F_lambda) = L_lambda.  OK
    - single side of slope lambda: apply Steps 1-3 to F_lambda (its own (ACCOUNT)
      is declared inherited). All of its roots have w_rho = lambda, so
      n_lambda(F_lambda) = deg F_lambda = D L_lambda and every other n is 0;
      by (SIDE-COUNT) at F_lambda, L_lambda(F_lambda) = L_lambda and all other
      lengths vanish.  OK -- so S1.7A's "whose lower hull is its single side of
      slope lambda" is a CONSEQUENCE, not an extra hypothesis, which is worth
      recording since the note presents it as part of the declared package.
    - strict degree decrease when there are >= 2 sides: each L_lambda > 0 and they
      sum to mu, so D L_lambda < D mu.  OK
    - reassembly: prod_lambda F_lambda = F with disjoint exhaustive root sets, so
      the children's terminal factorizations multiply to F's.  OK
  MULTI-SIDE SPLIT CONFIRMED as an induction case.

E.3  FINDING E-1 (minor) -- r3 repair 7's reconciliation of the direct clause-1/2
     read with the split-first algorithm cites the wrong clause.

      "> ... and labels attach to roots pointwise (S1.7, clause 1), so the label
      > classes read on the block \(F_\lambda\) are the same root sets as the
      > classes read directly at side \(\lambda\) of \(F\);"
  S1.7 clause 1 reads:
      "1. its roots lie in the intended disk and carry the label defined by
      \(r'\);"
  -- a statement about the roots of the TEST POLYNOMIAL Psi_{lambda,r'}, not about
  the roots of F. The clause that makes the label of a root of F pointwise is
  clause 2:
      "2. for roots of \(F\), its generic evaluation value is attained away from
      that label class and rises strictly on that class;"
  which characterises the class as {rho : d(Psi_{lambda,r'}(rho)) > generic} --
  determined by rho and (lambda, r') alone, since Psi_{lambda,r'} is supplied by
  the carrier from (lambda, r') and not from F ("For every side ... and every
  monic irreducible factor \(r'\) of \(R_\lambda\), the carrier supplies a monic
  polynomial \(\Psi_{\lambda,r'}\)"). With clause 2 substituted the argument does
  close: the direct partition and the block partition are restrictions of the same
  pointwise partition to the same root set, and since each is exhaustive their
  index sets coincide. The claim "The two routes agree" is therefore TRUE; the
  cited justification is not the one that supports it.
  Classification: minor.

E.4  Two smaller core observations, reported without classification

  (a) The orbit polynomial's integrality ("integral over O, hence in O[x]") is used
      in clause 2's last step but is nowhere stated in S1.7's test assertions
      (clause 7 covers "all tests and coefficient lifts", not orbit polynomials).
      It is immediate (roots are integral over the complete DVR O and the
      coefficients are Galois-fixed), and the sources supply it explicitly
      (HE7 L1750-1751, "f_C := Π_{ρ∈C}(x − ρ) has K₀-coefficients, integral over
      O, hence lies in O[x]"), so I do not raise it.
  (b) S1.7A's (LABEL-OWN) is exactly (RES-DEG) applied to the emitted block:
      deg R(F_{lambda,r'}) = nu/e' and the residual is c r'^k, so
      k deg r' = nu/e', i.e. k = nu/(e' deg r'). The two displays are the same
      identity at two scopes, which is a good sign for the re-route's coherence
      and I record it as a positive.

